<TopicPage slug="subjects/development-studies/foundational/what-is-development">

  <h2>Where you are, and what you will learn</h2>
  <p>
    This is the first lesson in Development Studies, and it asks the question the whole
    subject is built on: <em>what does it mean for a society to develop?</em> You need nothing
    from earlier lessons — no economics, no statistics beyond the idea of an average, no
    prior knowledge of any country. Everything is built here, step by step.
  </p>
  <p>By the end you will be able to:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>explain why a country's income, though easy to measure, cannot by itself define development;</li>
    <li>trace the field's main answers — modernisation theory, dependency theory and the capabilities approach — and say what each gets right;</li>
    <li>read GDP and the Human Development Index the way a development researcher does, blind spots included;</li>
    <li>use the Lorenz curve and the Gini coefficient to see the inequality that averages hide;</li>
    <li>explain why choosing a measure decides the policy — which is why measurement in this field is a political act.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    One habit runs through the page: every time a number is introduced, we ask first what it
    counts, and then who it hides. The counting is easy; the hiding is the lesson.
  </p>

  <h2>The question that starts the field</h2>
  <p>
    Compare two countries earning roughly the same income per head. In one, children are
    vaccinated, girls finish school, mothers rarely die in childbirth, and people live into
    their late seventies. In the other, an elite grows wealthy from oil while much of the
    population lacks clinics, sanitation and reliable schools. Which of them is developed?
    Money cannot answer — the two earn the same, yet the lives people can actually lead are
    utterly different. Development studies exists because that question needs better
    instruments than income alone.
  </p>
  <p>
    The modern answer comes from the Indian economist Amartya Sen, in his book
    <em>Development as Freedom</em> (1999). Sen's <strong>capabilities approach</strong>
    defines development as the expansion of what people can <em>be and do</em>: to be
    nourished and educated, to live long, to take part in public life, to speak without fear.
    Income matters because it buys these capabilities — but the conversion is not automatic.
    The same income buys mobility for someone who can walk and not for a wheelchair user in a
    city without pavements; it buys schooling far more easily for a boy than, in many places,
    for a girl. Income is a means, and its conversion into real lives varies enormously.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>So far:</strong> development is not a synonym for wealth. It is the expansion of
    what people can be and do — and the rest of this page turns that idea into concepts,
    measures and warnings.
  </p>

  <h2>Growth versus development: the first distinction</h2>
  <p>
    The field's opening distinction separates <strong>economic growth</strong> — more output
    year on year, counted as GDP rising — from <strong>development</strong>, a transformation
    in how people live. Growth is a quantity; development is a change in kind. A country can
    grow for decades while its people die young, if the gains concentrate in an enclave
    economy around oil wells or mines. And some celebrated successes did it the other way
    round: Costa Rica, the Indian state of Kerala and Sri Lanka achieved long, literate,
    healthy lives on modest incomes, by spending deliberately on public health and schooling.
    That is why this subject counts lives, not just output.
  </p>


  <h2>Two big answers from history</h2>
  <p>
    How does development happen? The first full theory was <strong>modernisation
    theory</strong>, dominant in the 1950s and 1960s. Its best-known statement is Walt
    Rostow's <em>The Stages of Economic Growth</em> (1960), which described one ladder every
    country climbs: traditional society, preconditions for take-off, take-off, drive to
    maturity, and the age of high mass consumption. Poor countries, on this view, need mainly
    investment and technology to follow the rich ones up. The theory was optimistic, and it
    missed something large: it treated each country as if it climbed alone.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Dependency theory</strong> answered from the global South. The world economy, it
    argued, is wired together: a rich "core" buys cheap raw materials from a poor
    "periphery" and sells back expensive manufactures, so the terms of trade themselves drain
    development away. Underdevelopment was not a rung anyone missed — it was <em>made</em>,
    by colonial extraction and unequal exchange. Neither theory won outright; the field now
    asks which institutions, which industrial strategies and which colonial histories
    separate the countries that converged on rich-world living standards from those that did
    not — and later units take those answers one at a time.
  </p>
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> "development just means becoming a copy of a rich Western
  country." It seems reasonable because modernisation theory drew exactly that single ladder,
  and rich countries do share much. But it is incomplete: countries reach long, healthy,
  educated lives by different routes — Costa Rica and Cuba on modest incomes through public
  provision, East Asia through export-led industry — and global trade ties poor countries'
  options to rich countries' choices. Development is the expansion of lives, not the imitation
  of one national template.</p>

  <h2>The capabilities answer, and its checklist</h2>
  <p>
    Sen's capabilities approach reframed the goal rather than the mechanism: the question is
    not "how much income?" but "what can this person actually do and be?". The philosopher
    Martha Nussbaum made the idea operational by listing central capabilities — life, health,
    education, political participation and more — that every society owes every person. The
    United Nations made the idea institutional: its first annual <em>Human Development
    Report</em> (1990) re-centred world statistics on lives rather than ledgers.
  </p>
  <p>
    One further broadening followed. <strong>Sustainable development</strong> added the time
    dimension, in the Brundtland Commission's 1987 formulation: meeting present needs without
    compromising the ability of future generations to meet theirs. The policy architecture
    then followed the idea — the Millennium Development Goals (2000, eight goals) and the
    Sustainable Development Goals (2015, seventeen goals and 169 targets) are capabilities
    thinking written as a checklist the whole world reports against. Notice the pattern: each
    refinement of the <em>definition</em> produced a new set of <em>targets</em>. That link
    is the subject's engine, and the widget below compresses it into five claims.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="measuring more than money" widget="equationStep" config={devCfg} />
  <p>
    <strong>What to notice:</strong> the five claims trace this page's argument in miniature.
    Claim 1 is the definition fight we just fought; claim 2 is the index we meet next; claim
    3 is the curve plotted in the second widget; claim 4 is the inequality section; claim 5
    is the punchline — choose the indicator and you choose the development project. Pause on
    claim 3: "the first dollars buy the most life" is the most policy-relevant sentence in
    this field.
  </p>

  <h2>Measuring development: what GDP counts, and what it misses</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Gross domestic product</strong> (GDP) is the market value of all final goods and
    services produced in a country in a period — usually a year. It earns its centrality by
    being countable and comparable: one number per nation, every year. But every word of the
    definition is also a limitation. <em>Market value</em>: anything without a price
    vanishes — unpaid care work and the informal economy, a large share of activity in poor
    countries and systematically the work of women. <em>Produced</em>: cutting a forest
    counts as income, and the loss of the forest counts as nothing. <em>Per capita</em> (when
    we divide by population): the mean knows nothing about who beneath it holds the money.
    And to compare countries at all, the numbers must be converted with <strong>purchasing
    power parity</strong> (PPP) — an adjustment that asks how much of the same basket of
    goods a unit of currency actually buys locally, because a dollar buys far more haircuts
    and rice in Lagos than in London.
  </p>
  <p>
    Before the sophisticated indices, the field's first world map was a single drawn line.
    The <strong>Brandt line</strong> (1980), named for the commission chaired by the former
    German chancellor Willy Brandt, wrapped the globe to separate a richer "Global North"
    from a poorer "Global South". It remains a useful starting image — wealth, health and
    technology do cluster north of it — but it over-generalises: Australia and New Zealand
    sit south of the line yet rank among the richest countries on Earth. Every map of
    development is a simplification you must be able to argue with.
  </p>

  <Widget no="2" caption="income and life expectancy — the Preston curve" widget="plotter" config={hdiCfg} />
  <p>
    <strong>What to notice:</strong> the curve rises steeply and then flattens. This is the
    <strong>Preston curve</strong> (named for the demographer Samuel Preston, 1975): life
    expectancy climbs sharply with income at low incomes and then levels off. The first
    dollars per head buy vaccines, sanitation, nutrition and maternal survival — cheap,
    transformative things; the next tens of thousands buy much less life. Just as important
    is what you cannot see on the line: the real world is a scatter <em>around</em> this
    curve. Countries far above it (strong public health on modest incomes — Cuba and Sri
    Lanka are the classics) and far below it (weak institutions on rich ones — several
    oil exporters) prove that income converts into health through policy, not automatically.
    Development policy is largely the study of moving a country <em>up</em> off the curve,
    not just along it.
  </p>

  <h2>The HDI: one number against GDP's blindness</h2>
  <p>
    The <strong>Human Development Index</strong> (HDI) is the field's flagship correction to
    GDP alone, published by the UN Development Programme since 1990. It bundles three
    dimensions into one score between 0 and 1: a long life (life expectancy at birth),
    knowledge (mean and expected years of schooling) and a decent standard of living (income
    per person, PPP-adjusted), combined as a <strong>geometric mean</strong> — an average
    that punishes a very low score in any one dimension, so a country cannot buy its way to a
    high HDI with money alone. The index is deliberately crude, and its value is diagnostic:
    it catches exactly the cases the definition fight predicted. Cuba and Mexico land far
    above what their incomes predict — Cuba's life expectancy matches that of the United
    States on a fraction of the income per head — while high-income Gulf states such as
    Kuwait land below theirs, because income was never the binding constraint on schooling
    and health.
  </p>
  <p>
    Beyond the HDI the toolkit multiplies by question. The <strong>Multidimensional Poverty
    Index</strong> (MPI, 2010) counts overlapping deprivations in health, education and
    living standards — nutrition, child mortality, schooling, cooking fuel, sanitation, clean
    water, electricity, flooring and assets — and identifies roughly a billion
    multidimensionally poor people in the countries it covers, many of whom income lines miss
    entirely. The <strong>inequality-adjusted HDI</strong> discounts the score for how
    unevenly each dimension is distributed, and the gender indices make the field's oldest
    lesson — that development is gendered — visible in the tables. The income line still does
    careful work: the World Bank's international extreme poverty line, now about 3 US dollars
    per person per day at 2021 purchasing-power parity, tracks the barest floor, while
    national poverty lines capture what each society considers minimally decent.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>So far:</strong> GDP counts the market; the Preston curve shows income buying life
    with diminishing returns; the HDI bundles three dimensions so a rich-but-sick country
    cannot flatter itself. But every one of these numbers is an average — and averages hide
    people. The next section is about learning to see them.
  </p>
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> "if GDP is rising, the population is becoming better
  off." It sounds reasonable because news reports treat growth as good news, and often it is.
  But GDP measures aggregate market activity, and it can rise through extracting resources,
  through rebuilding after disasters, through asset bubbles, and through gains that
  concentrate almost entirely at the top — while median incomes, life expectancy and
  security stagnate. That is why development studies treats GDP as one instrument reading
  among many, never as the patient's pulse.</p>

  <h2>Inequality within and between countries: averages hide people</h2>
  <p>
    National means conceal distributions, and the field's tool for seeing inside them is the
    <strong>Lorenz curve</strong> — a graph plotting the cumulative share of income held by
    the poorest cumulative share of the population. It is summarised in one number, the
    <strong>Gini coefficient</strong>: 0 means perfect equality (everyone earns the same) and
    100 means one person holds everything. Think of it as a measure of how far a country's
    income shape bends away from a perfectly flat line. The empirical spread is enormous:
    Nordic countries sit around the high 20s, the United States near 40, and the most unequal
    large countries — South Africa, Brazil — in the 50s and beyond. The levels matter because
    they interact with growth: the same growth reduces poverty much faster where inequality
    is lower, while high inequality converts growth into concentrated gains.
  </p>
  <p>
    Splitting global inequality into <em>between-country</em> and <em>within-country</em>
    parts is the modern headline finding. For most of the nineteenth and twentieth centuries,
    the gaps between countries dominated — the industrial revolution's great divergence. Since
    the 1990s the rise of Asia has narrowed between-country gaps while within-country
    inequality widened in much of the rich world; the economist Branko Milanovic's famous
    "elephant curve" traced both at once — the global middle class rising steeply while many
    lower-middle earners in rich countries stagnated. Meanwhile wealth has concentrated
    dramatically at the very top: by the middle 2010s the richest 1 percent of people held
    about half of all the world's wealth.
  </p>
  <p>
    Disaggregation is the discipline that follows. Poverty rates sliced by gender, region,
    ethnicity and age routinely reveal that a country "on track" for its aggregate goal
    contains populations decades behind it — rural girls versus urban boys is the classic,
    persistent slice. The contrasts can be stark inside one country: city dwellers in
    Shanghai live much like the average Italian, while many rural Chinese live closer to the
    average person in Sub-Saharan Africa. Two identical-GDP countries can differ wildly in
    who is poor, and that difference is precisely the development question: which
    institutions convert national income into widespread capability, and which convert it
    into enclaves.
  </p>
  <p>
    Sen's analysis of famine lands the same lesson where it hurts. Famine deaths track
    <strong>entitlements</strong> — who can command food through what they produce, own, earn
    or receive — not national food supplies; people starve while food exists in the country
    because their means of acquiring it collapse. And his survey found that major famines
    have not occurred in functioning democracies with a free press: public accountability
    converts information into response. Distribution is not a refinement of development;
    under the capabilities definition, it is the substance of it.
  </p>
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> "the average person earns the average income." The
  language invites it, but income distributions are skewed — a few very high earners pull
  the mean upward while most people earn less than it. That is exactly what the Lorenz curve
  and the Gini coefficient exist to expose, and it is why careful development statistics
  prefer the <em>median</em> (the person halfway up the queue) or report rates disaggregated
  by group. An average is a summary of a shape, not a portrait of a person.</p>

  <h2>From definition to project: development in practice</h2>
  <p>
    The definition fight ends in bank accounts. Because large lenders historically served the
    wealthy, small farmers in poor countries faced constant risk: no credit to buy seed,
    fertiliser or a second cow, and a single bad season could wipe them out.
    <strong>Microfinance</strong> — banking services such as small loans, savings accounts and
    insurance offered to people excluded from ordinary banks — grew from that gap. A loan of
    a few hundred dollars can double a farmer's herd and, with it, her income. Platforms such
    as Kiva.org match small individual lenders with borrowers directly, and reported
    repayment rates are strikingly high — near 97 percent, even among the poorest borrowers.
    The capabilities idea is visible inside the banking: what was missing was not effort but
    the <em>freedom</em> to borrow — and expanding that freedom is development by definition.
  </p>

  <h2>Check yourself</h2>
  <p>Try these before moving on. Feedback follows each answer.</p>
  <ol>
    <li>In your own words: what does the capabilities approach say development is?</li>
    <li>Give two things GDP does not count that clearly affect how people live.</li>
    <li>Countries A and B have the same GDP per capita. Name one measurement that would tell them apart, and say what it might show.</li>
    <li>How can famine occur in a country that is not at war and produces enough food?</li>
  </ol>
  <p>
    <strong>Feedback:</strong> (1) Development is the expansion of what people can be and do —
    health, education, voice, security — with income as a means, not the goal itself; if your
    answer was only "more money", you have GDP's view, not Sen's. (2) Unpaid care and
    household work (no market price) and the depletion of forests, water and soils (counted as
    income, never as cost); the informal economy and distribution are also acceptable. (3) The
    HDI or life expectancy at birth would separate them — an oil exporter with GDP to match
    Europe but weak schooling and clinics lands far below a Costa Rica on the same income; the
    Gini coefficient would reveal whether the income is shared. (4) Because famine tracks
    entitlements, not supplies: people's means of acquiring food — wages, crops, assets,
    transfers — can collapse while food remains available, which is why accountability and a
    free press, not just food aid, are part of the answer.
  </p>

  <h2>Key takeaways</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Development is the expansion of what people can be and do (Sen's capabilities approach); income is a means whose conversion into lives varies.</li>
    <li>Growth is more output; development is a transformation in how people live — the two can move in opposite directions.</li>
    <li>Modernisation theory pictured one ladder all countries climb; dependency theory argued the ladder is rigged by unequal trade and colonial history.</li>
    <li>Sustainable development (Brundtland, 1987) adds the future: present needs without compromising future generations.</li>
    <li>GDP's blind spots: unpaid and informal work, natural-capital depletion, distribution, and honest cross-country comparison needs PPP.</li>
    <li>The Preston curve flattens: the first dollars per head buy the most life — and countries sit above or below the curve through policy, not income.</li>
    <li>The HDI bundles life expectancy, schooling and income; the MPI, IHDI and gender indices each expose what GDP and HDI hide.</li>
    <li>The Lorenz curve and Gini coefficient reveal the distribution averages conceal — and disaggregation finds the people averages hide.</li>
    <li>Choosing the measure chooses the target: measurement in this field is policy by other means.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Where this goes next</h2>
  <p>
    This page defined the goal and its instruments. The next unit asks the harder question:
    <em>why do the gaps exist?</em> — the historical causes (colonialism and trade), the
    geographical and institutional ones, and the mechanics of debt and dependency. Later
    units take up the economic dimensions (agriculture, industry, aid and investment), the
    social dimensions (education, health, gender), governance, and finally the Sustainable
    Development Goals in practice — and each uses a tool built here, so when a future page
    reports an MPI by region or an adjusted HDI, you will know what it counts and whom it
    was designed to stop hiding.
  </p>

  <h2>Going deeper</h2>
  <p>
    Why the PPP fuss? A worked example makes the adjustment concrete. A nurse earning the
    local equivalent of 6,000 US dollars a year in a low-income country and a nurse earning
    30,000 dollars in a rich one are five worlds apart at market exchange rates — but closer
    to two apart at purchasing-power parity, because haircuts, staples, housing and bus fares
    cost a fraction of rich-world prices. Multiplied across whole economies, the adjustment
    moves hundreds of millions of people across the "poverty" threshold on paper without a
    single income changing. Hence the field's health warning: the <em>trends</em> in poverty
    are robust, the <em>levels</em> are conventions, and any argument that turns on whether a
    population sits just above or just below a line is usually an argument about the line.
  </p>
  <p>
    Three deeper debates carry this foundation into current research. <strong>The politics of
    measurement</strong>: what gets measured gets managed, so indicator choice is itself
    power — GDP's blind spots, the HDI's omission of freedom (Sen's own caveat), and the
    SDGs' 169 targets as a negotiation artefact all show it; "beyond GDP" attempts, from
    natural-capital accounts to Bhutan's decision to steer by Gross National Happiness, are
    attempts to re-run the founding definition fight with better instruments. <strong>The
    convergence question</strong>: why East Asian economies reached rich-world living
    standards in two generations while much of Sub-Saharan Africa and Latin America did not —
    institutions, industrial policy, geography, colonial legacies, conflict — remains the
    field's organising puzzle, and honest answers are multicausal and contested.
    <strong>Growth itself</strong>: the climate century has reopened whether universal
    prosperity fits within planetary boundaries, splitting the field between green-growth
    optimists and post-growth thinkers who argue rich economies must redefine prosperity
    below GDP's ceiling. What is not contested is what this page built: development is about
    the expansion of lives, not ledgers — and every average in this discipline is hiding
    someone whose development has not started.
  </p>

